        "ev:e7ad9b4154ddcae33093bbe8aa5e797096a1c41fc078a9b4501f6651baf348a5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:554b795dfd28a21b85a753509f5a53deab07e369b00cd1ab72d34a1a1270da21",
      "text": "On visual-art therapy in Parkinson's disease, an observational, prospective, open-label, exploratory study of eighteen patients reported improved Navon test, eye-tracking and UPDRS performance after twenty sessions of art therapy, whereas the authors of an art-therapy meta-analysis state that visual arts and painting represent a significant research gap whose direct therapeutic effects remain unexplored. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33526323/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12444631/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:25913b81a2265a8d8198ee6b53c77e4d9f27755fb1df73ac62857ed3fbe6b68f",
        "ev:6f16ef71db1657e8c4a0ce258d587e876a6dfb0485281a0cf17d393d9f9616f8",
        "ev:b8b7348fa8ddb7e370553fd2f29086626e30a14c2ee3d1126c900e3d07e5c583",
        "ev:f30f980084c02a88c3e53527e3eddc680f1bda5c9c186e1c6703afe8dbffacb2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:de07fcbbbf986b3d9affa1a9138e1c46d1c80296a549150f06424a90582ecad3",
      "text": "In the ParkinSong controlled trial of 75 people living with Parkinson's disease, which compared a singing-based intervention with an active control condition at 2 dosage levels (weekly vs monthly) over 3 months, intervention participants showed significant improvements in vocal intensity, maximum expiratory pressure and voice-related quality of life compared with controls, with no statistical differences between groups on maximum phonation length or maximum inspiratory pressure at 3 months; a systematic review describes this trial as a non-randomised controlled trial. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31081485/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7547481/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:3f0a4d323437a789067cd26d47d2483089d9412410480be634d1a5f7abe5e25f",
        "ev:65c8abb48ef5ec9e82d42f71278e95be3cb0eb5fadc28d81c563b4669e8e795c",
        "ev:863726cd9fb6b4f238a88cd6e8bed9dd3d388e5bd928cc371b3691788f6a0610",
        "ev:ac47ada77cfa671f414bcb24149f810f4a93b7b67abf9c6d509a0ca397d5e01a",
        "ev:d6c46c2db1894c8ebaaf6c1d7dd16f6874f1135b3b9ac65da3b8279aa143b89c"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:c4508db920dca5fdc1bd2760f0b0fff118fa4ea9f4217b3a23ed8580171797fa",
      "text": "In the 'art therapy' meta-analysis by Liu and colleagues of 26 randomized controlled trials (2,756 individuals), the included interventions were 5 music, 14 dance, 2 yoga, 1 mindfulness, 1 theater, 1 virtual-reality game-based, 1 Tai Chi plus yoga and 1 Qigong plus dance studies, and the authors conclude that dance therapy exhibited the most pronounced effects across motor function outcomes. (low confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12444631/",
      "also": [],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
